{"content":"Found the regression: in `sqlparse/sql.py`, `TokenList._token_matching` computes the reversed-search index range as `range(start - 2, -2, -1)`. With Python's negative indexing, this lets the loop reach index `-1`, which wraps around and incorrectly matches the *last* token in the list instead of correctly stopping when no earlier token exists (i.e., returning None as `token_prev` should when at the start of a list). The correct upstream bound is `-1` (exclusive stop), so the walk correctly stops after index `0`.

This single off-by-one explains the batch of reported failures: anything relying on `token_prev`/`token_next(reverse=True)` behaving correctly near the beginning of a token list (comment stripping logic that inspects `prev_`, the reindent splitter's statement-boundary checks via `token_prev`, etc.) could spuriously see a "previous token" that's actually the tail of the list, corrupting whitespace insertion/removal decisions.

Applying the one-line fix.","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_1","name":"replace_text","arguments":{"path":"sqlparse/sql.py","old_text":"            indexes = range(start - 2, -2, -1)","new_text":"            indexes = range(start - 2, -1, -1)"}}]}
